Seriously, you might want to use the /m (match) option in future so you 
are offered bones of the same level as the ones you're uploading.  Of 
course, this does mean that you won't be gaining extra bones files, but 
you can't have everything.

Now as requested, more than anyone would really want to know about how 
Hearse handles bones.
==

Hearse offers files a variable number of times depending on how many 
bones exist for that particular version.  Unused bones files eventually 
expire off the server (after 12 months) unless deleting them would cause 
there to be too few bones (<200) available for that particular version.

Bones files that have been downloaded are kept hanging around for a good 
while in case anyone runs into them and finds a problem - I need to be 
able to investigate it later, so which have been downloaded the maximum 
number of times are marked 'not for download' and kept for another six 
months.  It's slightly more complicated than that, but I'm boring myself 
just writing this so I won't go into detail.

Now, protection against recycling bones:  All bones files are referenced 
by a MD5 hash of their contents; all deleted bones have their MD5 hash 
added to a table called 'AncientBones'.  Any bones that are offered to 
the server have their MD5 checked against active bones and ancient bones 
and rejected accordingly.

Time stamps: On the client side, in Windows Hearse stuffs the 'last 
updated' date in the registry, and I believe the Unix client plonks it 
in the config file.

Cheating Hearse:  Hearse isn't astonishingly secure, but it does have a 
nice repertoire of scripts that I think filter out almost all 
unintentional files.  Certainly, I've had no complaints about any bad 
Hearse bones for at least a year, if not more.  Please note, this is NOT 
a challenge to any l33t people out there - I know that preventing ANY 
bad bones files entering Hearse is a practical impossibility, all I'm 
trying to do is keep 'em to a minimum.  If anyone ever managed to swamp 
Hearse with bad bones files, all I'd do is shut the server down.  The 
defence that is no defence.  Very Zen.

For the interested though, mainly Hearse keeps an eye on patterns of 
use.  People who consistently upload *only* high level bones files 
(probable savescummers), people who *consistently* attempt to upload 
wizard bones (grr!) and so on are eventually marked as 'untrustworthy'.  
The upshot of this is that none of the bones files they submit are seen 
by anyone else unless I've manually vetted them first.  Since I never 
have the time to do this, the world is a happy place.  The files that 
'Untrustworthy' users are offered in return for their bones aren't 
counted towards bone expiration and so they are effectively sandboxed 
and can have no effect on any other user.  I'm then notified and go and 
check it out - there's only been a couple of occasions when I've 
reinstated the user.  As far as the 'Untrustworthy' user goes, he sees 
absolutely no difference in Hearse's behaviour.

It's fairly rare that I ban people (or have to) - I have to be convinced 
that they're deliberately trying to cause trouble.  They have to do 
something fairly egregious to get banned when ignoring them is so much 
simpler.

Finally, Kent, I think reusing bones files is horrible, but what 
consenting adults do outside Hearse is none of my business. :)

